    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21701,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21700,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "“BROWN CLAWS BACK” reads the <i>Daily Mail</i> headline the next day, whilst most of the focus is on Labour, extending the rule of a largely unpopular leader isn’t going to reflect well on you."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21702,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21686,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Progressives across the country decry this decision. \"TORY ENABLER\" they scream, but governing with Scottish Nationalists would never work and you know it. Gordon knows it too, so the deal collapses."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21704,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21703,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Quite a scandalous stance you just took. Every paper in the country now presents you as a pretentious brat, and the crown you had on your head only a few days ago is falling – in the mud, shall I say. This will reduce your apparent credibility heading into the next Parliament."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21705,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21687,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "You’ve gone in swinging and Cameron agrees to give you control over Defence whilst losing out on the great offices of state, you’ve won but could you have got more?"
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21707,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21706,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "When the call from Cameron comes, you’ve been offered Business, Energy, and Scotland, with more junior roles also being yours. It may be underwhelming, but it’s the only deal you’ve got. Welcome to Downing St. Nick."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21708,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21688,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Labour is battered, so by subtly pushing yourself as an alternative, you’re raising your parties profile. Let’s just hope you stick to the image of “moderation”, and not “subservience”."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21710,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21709,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "You sound like Cameron during the campaign, whilst there’s a certain amount of support you can gain from this. Don’t alienate Labour yet, you may need them in 5 years… Or sooner."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21729,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21728,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "This is simple... But you’re advocating for a voting system a lot of people don’t understand, you’re gambling a lot on people grasping it quickly, and there are plenty of simplistic ways to oppose it. You may have just shot yourself in the foot Nick."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21731,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21730,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "If the referendum passes, you’ll go down in history as the man who may have broken the 2-party system in Britain. Yet many think it won’t pass so you leave no part unexplained. Let’s hope the people are on your side."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21733,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21732,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "While this is your expected stance, you’re going against your own government line, which is currently emphasizing the need to continue the inquiry on the legal status of the war. Showing divisiness on a subject like this, especially when we still have soldiers in Afghanistan, isn’t the way to go."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21735,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21734,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "At your barbs with the opposition, Shadow Secretary of Justice Jack Straw points to a poll showing wide-spread cynicism about your significance in the government – in LibDem seats. But by-and-large, you deflect the attack effectively. You better show results in a few years down the road though, and put your money where your mouth is."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21737,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21736,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "This would’ve been a devious plan, but it does eventually leak to the Times. When Cameron finds out, you have a massive row. While the coalition is intact for now, be careful not to upset the apple-cart too much, or we could be looking at an early election, especially with Cameron’s approval ratings."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21739,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21738,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "The blowback is immediate. Labour MPs, led by their new leader Ed Miliband, are quick to criticize the term limits calling it overlong. You’ve stabilized relations with the front bench for now, but even some Tory backbenchers are calling the bill ‘profoundly cynical’."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21741,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21740,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "This is a radical declaration that only the right wing of the Tory party is advocating for. The look of betrayal in Kennedy’s face haunts you for weeks, Regardless, Cameron chooses to go to the UN, but does heed your advice for strong military support, especially to protect British interests."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21743,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21742,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "This is the most mainstream idea. There is plenty of support for a coalition of the willing on an international level to take out Gaddafi, and it makes it much easier to sell in the Commons for debate."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21745,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21744,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "This is not the most popular option. While the Lib Dems do have a history of opposing military intervention (Iraq springs to mind), this is not good for the coalition at all. The Tory ministers all support going to the UN, and while you do have a point about the democracy aspect, most want to get rid of Gaddafi and let the chips fall where they may."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21747,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21746,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Osborne notes your displeasure with the BoE regulation and agrees to a longer timetable, but he gives an evasive answer on the number for austerity. You recognize that he’s probably getting orders from someone higher up the food chain. Because of this, you probably won’t be able to make any adjustments to the budget at all. Be prepared for some tough conversations with sitting MPs."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21749,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21748,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "David and Danny are able to ensure some minor concessions, but for the most part, the budget stays where it is. Not the worst strategy, especially considering the Tories are not very happy with the more moderating tendencies your party has."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21751,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21750,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Negotiations are initially uneventful, but then the bombshell drops. An unnamed Lib Dem MP leaks the burden transfer plan to The Guardian. When this happens, a furious Osborne breaks off all communication with you and says the budget is staying where it is with no concessions. Dejected, you and Vince Cable start to get the campaign wheels turning. An election is coming soon, whether you want it or not."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21753,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21752,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "You should’ve known better Nick. It looked like you were going to escape, but once former leader Menzies Campbell said he would defy the whip, some 30 MPs voted against. Your reputation is in tatters, as university towns are swinging drastically against you. Good luck."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21755,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21754,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Very interesting. While this doesn’t go as far as many of the uni voters wanted, it is enough to placate your MPs, and you’re able to get a bill that passes with some Tory support as well. While you do take a hit in university towns, they don’t see you as an enemy. Best of both worlds?"
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21757,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21756,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Your seismic announcement sends shockwaves through the British political landscape. The SNP is well and truly behind you, with even a tentative majority of Labour MPs supporting you as well. But one place you won’t find refuge is the Tory benches. And they are furious. Cameron maintains the coalition for now, but Pride goes before the fall, Nick."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21759,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21758,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Menzies flatly refuses. With him unwilling, there aren’t many figures who could pressure the PM into making a change. The Tories appoint one of their own, and your own backbenchers excoriate you for your opportunism and lack of tact."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21761,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21760,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Smart strategy. You and the PM sit down for a two-hour conversation and near the end, you propose bringing an LD into the fold as comms director. While the PM doesn’t commit to it, he does agree to not bring in a member of the Eurosceptic wing. A nominal win, it seems, can be touted."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21763,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21762,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Very conservative of you Nick. While you are able to spend some time rallying voters and getting them excited about the progress you’re making, you feel hollow. Deep down you and your backbenchers know if you can’t flip the tide, it could be a harsh next election."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21765,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21764,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "You’re able to run an effective damage control by enlisting backbench Tory MPs to argue on Vince’s behalf. While you’ve quenched the flames for now, expect the next time an LD frontbencher runs his or her mouth to be much more difficult. "
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21767,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21766,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Parts of the frontbench agree with this stance, and the PM is satisfied with your reasoning. However, the PM does remove the media oversight privilege from Vince, and he’s none too pleased. Neither are the LD MPs, and you’re excoriated yet again for your lack of backbone when it comes to standing up to the Tories."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21769,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21768,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Oh Nick. You should’ve known better. Tory frontbenchers, eager to even score after the leaks, leak the meeting details with apology enclosed to friendly papers. When this gets out, you have a very uncomfortable PMQ session where Ed Miliband formally calls for elections to ‘end this farce of a Tory government’. You endure embarrassment from the Tories too."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21771,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21770,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Thankfully, this promise was not very high on the list of things you promised. While you launch a renewed salvo on housing costs and affordability, only the high-profile economists in London speculate about the plan. And after '07-'08, who’s really going to listen to them anyway?"
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21773,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21772,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Cunning, Nick. It’s all hands on deck, as you quietly chat up Whip McLoughlin, Danny meets with Miliband, and Moore goes to the SNP. Remarkably, none of this leaks and you’re able to get something passed. For the first (and possibly last) time as DPM, you feel as if you’ve accomplished something."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21775,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21774,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "The reaction is rather muted when you make this announcement. Even as leaks from 10 Downing Street decry the costs attached and Williams spars with Tories in the press, there’s just no real majority to get anything passed. Osborne indicates he’s willing to hear you out, but that’s the end of the matter."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21777,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21776,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "You go along to the next One-Nation Club Dinner, they welcome you, they listen and by the end, you’ve secured the votes of the bloc. This will barely push you over the line to get the bill passed, but you’ve earned the respect of a core part of the Tories so that has to count for something surely?"
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21779,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21778,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Interesting strategy. This weirdly brings together people from many sections of the spectrum with Lib Dems, Labour, the SNP, even some Tories back this led by the MP for South Staffordshire Gavin Williamson. There’s some complaints from clergymen and veteran conservatives but the bill passes comfortably, nice thinking Nick."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21781,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21780,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Except he doesn’t because passing Lords reform was part of <i>your agenda</i>. You’ve been played by Cameron again, who makes up a half-assed attempt to get his party on side but in the end it fails. You can whine on TV as much as you like, but you were played like a two-pound fiddle."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21783,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21782,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "This is the safest bet to preserve the coalition. But you’re ceding tremendous ground here to both Labour and the SNP in university towns, and quietly your own MPs whisper that you could’ve done more."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21785,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21784,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "You dispatch Kennedy to the BBC to debate Farage and Labour’s John Prescott and it goes over… Surprisingly well. Kennedy lets Prescott hammer Farage over the inhumane parts of the denial, whilst Kennedy portrays us as the common-sense alternative. Not a bad piece of work."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21787,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21786,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "You put out a stronger than expected denunciation, but May avoids the meeting. Despite the stated purpose being for infrastructure advancements for uni towns, May susses out the real purpose. No harm, no foul, but be wary of Labour potentially stealing your shine."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21789,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21788,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Danny Alexander and Charles Kennedy stand side by side with Darling and Brown as if the last 4 years had never happened, but their presence certainly adds weight to this, maybe this is slightly cynical considering your vote share in Scotland but nevertheless it works, Scotland votes to remain in the union 55-45. The UK lives to fight another day."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21791,
        "fields": {
